Date: Wed, 6 Jan 93 15:31:59 EST
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: Re: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

Here's a screwy idea I am tossing out in the same vein as all the
Von Neumann machine/ E.T. thread.

Why is everybody so wrapped up in hard tech? Why not soft tech.
Imagine an alien race trying to notify the universe of its
existence. There are two major problems. Distance and time.
Other civs can be quite far away in distance and in their evolutionary
state. In order to solve this problem, they could leave messages encoded
in bio-molecules and in living organisms.

If a message were encoded in the DNA of a species of living organism,
 it would be altered at a relatively constant rate. Future generations
can work back and find the original message. Also, inserting multiple
copies or tying it in to an essential protein would help increase
its viability.

My fantasy is that when the Human Genome Project is completed,
we will find an open party invitation to an instellar bash at
Alpha Centauri. (Hopefully it is not BYOB)

This method of transmission eliminates the time factor. The message
is "sent" whenever in a planet's history and will be read by
the generations of sentient species when they reach the appropiate
level. On problem is that, unless you use some kind of panspermia,
the message must be hand-delivered.

Some people (notably Freeman Dyson) has proposed bio-engineering
as a much more feasible method of instellar probing. He lectured
here at OSU a number of years ago and talked about his "space chicken."
Basically, you engineer a living organism that combines desirable traits
like photosynthesis and mobility with non-organic components to
enable it to survive extreme condistions. Presumably if it
could reproduce, it would be some type of Von Neumann machine,
but would less of a machine than an actual creature.

