Krzsys (sp?) writes:

> I respectfully disagree with Perry, I think that advanced civilization,
> especially with commercial biological immortality, is incompatible with
> computer viruses or similar devices meant to harm others. 
> What is a Von Neuman machine, please ...

1. There is no evidence that technological sophistication means
harmful devices will be left undeveloped. I cite history for many,
many examples. You may, of course, believe that "advanced" (whatever
that means) civilizations will somehow avoid these areas, but I
consider this a wish, a speculation.

2. Von Neumann machines are self-replicating machines/factories.
Imagine one landing on a planet...some time later there are two such
machines (or 4 or 8 or whatever). These machines then travel out in
various directions and repeat the process. In some fairly small number
of years (hundreds of thousands to millions), much of the galaxy is
filled with these self-replicating machines. The implication for the
Fermi paradox ("Why aren't they here?") applies as well to these von
Neumann machines.

It seems inevitable that bright people (or aliens) will experiment
with these and other technologies, some of which may look like weapons
or have dangerous side effects.

Your hopes that advanced civilizations will not work on
self-reproducing things (which is essentially what computer viruses,
worms, and Drexler's "gray goo" are) seem unrealistic.

Besides, even in advanced civilizations there may be territorial
disputes, sociopaths, and common criminals, for which defense is needed.


